	faithBodyHtml={`<p>Statistics Netherlands (CBS) reports that in <strong>2023</strong>, <strong>58%</strong> of the Dutch had no religion, <strong>17%</strong> were Roman Catholic, <strong>13%</strong> Protestant and <strong>6%</strong> Muslim. In 1849 the picture was nearly the reverse: <strong>2%</strong> had no religion and <strong>56%</strong> were Protestant.</p><p>The Dutch Bible is old here. The Liesveltbijbel was printed in 1526, the Statenvertaling followed in 1637, and the Dutch Bible Society&rsquo;s translation of 1951 largely replaced it as the Protestant standard.</p><p>Dutch is also spoken in Belgium. In the European Social Survey 2023, 59% of Belgians said they are non-religious and 31% Catholic (as summarised on Wikipedia&rsquo;s <em>Religion in Belgium</em>); that figure covers all of Belgium, not Flanders alone.</p><p>The invitation of Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible&trade; is a personal one: read the same Word in clear Dutch, and meet in it a Father.</p>`}
